CREATED:20260913T201324Z
DESCRIPTION:Photographer and Duke MFA|EDA alumna Cassandra Klos explores h
 umanity's relationship to space\, scientific exploration\, and the possib
 ility of life beyond Earth in "Cosmic Origins\," an exhibition on view at
  CDS October 22 - December 18\, 2026.\n\nUPDATED GALLERY HOURS\n\nNovembe
 r 24 - 29: Closed to the public.\nNovember 29 - December 3: 9 a.m.-5 p.m.
 \nDecember 4: 9 a.m.-4 p.m. Last day of regular public gallery hours.\nDe
 cember 5 - 18: Closed to regular public visitation\, with limited weekday
  appointments\, subject to staff availability. Please email docstudies@du
 ke.edu.\n\nBringing together photographs\, artifacts\, and ephemera from 
 simulated space missions\, the exhibition features work from Klos's long-
 term project "Mars on Earth\," which documents her experiences as an anal
 og astronaut participating in Earth-based simulations designed to replica
 te the conditions\, challenges\, and isolation of space exploration.\n\nW
 ith pressurized spacesuits\, freeze-dried food\, robotic companions\, and
  Mission Control waiting back at "home\," Klos's photographs visualize wh
 at life beyond Earth might look and feel like for the first cosmic explor
 ers. The exhibition also includes spacesuits and materials from Klos's fi
 eldwork\, as well as work originally produced for National Geographic tha
 t will be exhibited together in the CDS Kreps Gallery for the first time.
 \n\nBlurring the boundaries between documentary evidence and speculative 
 imagination\, "Cosmic Origins" invites visitors to consider not only how 
 humans might live on the Moon or Mars\, but also what space exploration c
 an teach us about life on Earth.\n\nMore info about the exhibition: https
 ://documentarystudies.duke.edu/upcoming-exhibitions. Organized by CDS in 
 collaboration with Duke University's MFA|EDA program and the Duke SPACE I
 nitiative\, with support from Duke's Office of Interdisciplinary Programs
 .
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261218T050000
