CREATED:20260805T113449Z
DESCRIPTION:The Sperling Center for Jewish Studies at Duke University is p
 leased to present its Undergraduate Documentary Film Festival\, featuring
  original documentary films created by Duke undergraduates exploring Jewi
 sh history\, culture\, religion\, memory\, and contemporary Jewish life.\
 n\nDrawing on archival research\, oral histories\, interviews\, and perso
 nal narratives\, student filmmakers bring Jewish Studies scholarship to l
 ife through the medium of film. This year's projects explore the history 
 and contemporary experiences of Morocco's Sephardic Jewish community and 
 the complex dynamics of Jewish-Muslim relations\, as well as the story of
  the family of Keith Siegel\, who was taken hostage during the October 7 
 attacks. Through these films\, students examine questions of identity\, m
 emory\, resilience\, intercommunal relations\, and the ways global events
  resonate within local communities\, including at Duke and in Durham.\n\n
 The festival celebrates the creativity and intellectual engagement of Duk
 e undergraduates while highlighting the interdisciplinary nature of Jewis
 h Studies. By combining rigorous research with visual storytelling\, stud
 ents offer fresh perspectives on Jewish experiences past and present and 
 invite audiences to consider how documentary film can deepen our understa
 nding of history\, culture\, and collective memory.\n\nFollowing the scre
 enings\, audience members will have an opportunity to engage with the stu
 dent filmmakers in a discussion about their research\, filmmaking process
 \, and the broader questions raised by their work.\n\nDinner will precede
  the event at 6pm in the Ruby Lounge.  This event is free and open to the
  public.
DURATION:PT2H
DTSTAMP:20260806T134919Z
